# Atlas sync schedule. Each line hits POST $ATLAS_API/api/sync/<source>; the API
# records every attempt in sync_runs, so this file stays dumb on purpose.
#
# m  h  dom mon dow  command

# Heartbeat — proves cron can reach the API and write. Safe to drop once real
# sources are scheduled below.
5    *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh noop

# Phase 2 — weather, hourly. At :07, not :00 — the top of the hour is when
# every cron job on the internet hits Open-Meteo's free tier at once, and a
# :00 run can meet "503 The service is overloaded".
7    *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh weather

# Phase 3 — Securo reachability. A live proxy writes nothing; this only keeps
# a dated row in sync_runs so the Sources strip can say "live" or "unreachable".
*/15 *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh finance

# Phase 4 — Audiobookshelf reachability. Live proxy, writes nothing; same job
# as the finance line, on the same quarter-hour.
*/15 *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh listening

# Phase 6 — Canvas assignments, hourly. The source is named `school`, the key
# the dashboard reads, not `canvas`.
15   *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh school

# Phase 7 — Fitbit via Google Health API, hourly rather than the nightly the
# plan sketched: last night only exists once the watch has synced after you
# wake, and a 4am run would leave the card a night behind all day. ~6 calls a
# run, against a 300/minute per-user quota. Named `sleep`, the dashboard's key.
40   *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/sync.sh sleep
